WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.400
If you’re anything like me,

00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:06.560
you have lots of ambitious
plans for the future.

00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:09.160
I’ve got a whole series of
new books I want to write,

00:00:09.160 --> 00:00:11.400
thousands more
videos to script.

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:13.360
But guess what?

00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:16.760
None of us may be able to do
any of the things we want to do

00:00:16.760 --> 00:00:20.240
if we don't have our health.

00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:22.480
Welcome to the
NutritionFacts Podcast.

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:25.200
I’m your host,
Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:27.880
Today we explore the effect
of collagen on the body

00:00:27.880 --> 00:00:31.207
and we start with a story
about collagen supplements.

00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:35.120
Collagen supplements have become
quite the trendy treatment

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:36.280
for aging skin,

00:00:36.280 --> 00:00:39.400
available in an array of pills,
powders, and products

00:00:39.400 --> 00:00:43.440
from bars and gummies
to collagen coffee and beer.

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:46.240
Social media is said to be
"inundated with paid ads

00:00:46.240 --> 00:00:49.080
marketing
unsubstantiated claims."

00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:53.000
What claims, if any,
can be substantiated?

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:55.800
The word collagen comes
from the Greek kola,

00:00:55.800 --> 00:00:58.520
meaning "gum,"
and gen, meaning "producing."

00:00:58.520 --> 00:01:02.720
Its gelling nature is
exemplified by Jell-O gelatin,

00:01:02.720 --> 00:01:05.680
which is basically
just cooked collagen.

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:07.480
It is our most abundant protein

00:01:07.480 --> 00:01:08.920
and the main
structural component

00:01:08.920 --> 00:01:10.720
of most of the tissues
in our body.

00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:13.360
For example, up to three
quarters of our skin by weight

00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:15.360
may be pure collagen.

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:17.200
There are actually
dozens of different types,

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:18.600
but they all share
a characteristic

00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:22.280
triple helix configuration
of coiled subunits.

00:01:22.280 --> 00:01:24.960
What happens when we eat it?

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:27.880
If someone would have asked
for my educated guess

00:01:27.880 --> 00:01:29.160
about taking collagen

00:01:29.160 --> 00:01:33.000
before I took a deep dive
for my book How Not to Age,

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:36.720
I would have dismissed it
as witch-doctory woo-woo.

00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:40.840
Eating skin for your face would
seem to make as much sense

00:01:40.840 --> 00:01:42.760
as eating brains
to make yourself smarter

00:01:42.760 --> 00:01:45.240
or eyeballs
to improve your vision.

00:01:45.240 --> 00:01:47.040
After all,
when you eat proteins,

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:50.080
they are broken down into
their constituent amino acids,

00:01:50.080 --> 00:01:53.800
which are then rebuilt into
any protein your body needs.

00:01:53.800 --> 00:01:56.120
And, of all proteins to eat,

00:01:56.120 --> 00:01:58.000
collagen is the only protein
in our food supply

00:01:58.000 --> 00:01:59.520
that's truly incomplete.

00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:02.360
That is, it's missing
an essential amino acid.

00:02:02.360 --> 00:02:05.080
Other dietary proteins,
including all plant proteins,

00:02:05.080 --> 00:02:07.880
contain all of
the essential amino acids--

00:02:07.880 --> 00:02:10.440
in fact, that's why
they're called essential,

00:02:10.440 --> 00:02:13.280
since animals like us
can't make them.

00:02:13.280 --> 00:02:16.800
Collagen, though, is missing
the amino acid tryptophan.

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:20.480
So, the only protein source that
you couldn't live on is Jell-O,

00:02:20.480 --> 00:02:24.640
since it effectively has
a protein quality score of zero.

00:02:24.640 --> 00:02:26.960
But obviously that's
not all people eat;

00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:28.560
so, significant
amounts of collagen

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:31.160
could be incorporated into
the diet without keeling over,

00:02:31.160 --> 00:02:33.440
but tryptophan is special.

00:02:33.440 --> 00:02:36.720
Randomizing people
to even a single meal

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:38.960
of a gelatin-based protein drink

00:02:38.960 --> 00:02:41.760
can lead to memory impairments
within hours

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:45.640
due to
"acute tryptophan depletion,"

00:02:45.640 --> 00:02:48.520
presumably due to a drop
in the brain of serotonin,

00:02:48.520 --> 00:02:51.160
which is made from
tryptophan and doesn't just

00:02:51.160 --> 00:02:54.240
regulate mood but learning
and memory as well.

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:56.120
So, the idea
of collagen supplements

00:02:56.120 --> 00:02:57.640
never made sense to me,

00:02:57.640 --> 00:02:59.880
but the proof is in the pudding,

00:02:59.880 --> 00:03:03.200
or rather, perhaps,
the Jell-O.

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:06.840
What does the science say?

00:03:06.840 --> 00:03:08.960
My assumption that
collagen proteins would get

00:03:08.960 --> 00:03:12.480
completely broken down in
the digestive tract was wrong.

00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:15.120
Feed people gelatin from
pig skin or chicken feet,

00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:17.280
and little pieces
of collagen particles

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:20.480
can be found floating in
their bloodstream within hours.

00:03:20.480 --> 00:03:23.600
Though no further tracking has
been done in humans, in rodents,

00:03:23.600 --> 00:03:25.760
collagen fragments
from gelatin consumption

00:03:25.760 --> 00:03:29.120
can end up in
cartilage and skin.

00:03:29.120 --> 00:03:31.160
Now, biologically,
there is no mechanism

00:03:31.160 --> 00:03:33.880
by which these fragments
could be directly incorporated

00:03:33.880 --> 00:03:35.400
into new collagen,

00:03:35.400 --> 00:03:37.280
but there may be an indirect way

00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:40.080
collagen bits could boost
collagen deposition,

00:03:40.080 --> 00:03:42.320
as appears to happen
in rat skin.

00:03:42.320 --> 00:03:44.720
What about in people?

00:03:44.720 --> 00:03:48.080
Some of the collagen studies
lack control groups.

00:03:48.080 --> 00:03:51.560
I'm reminded of that vintage
Star Trek episode Mudd's Women,

00:03:51.560 --> 00:03:55.120
where Kirk successfully swaps
out a youth-giving supplement

00:03:55.120 --> 00:03:58.560
for a placebo
without the aliens noticing.

00:03:58.560 --> 00:04:02.200
Randomized controlled trials
are scarce,

00:04:02.200 --> 00:04:04.760
but a systematic review
and meta-analysis

00:04:04.760 --> 00:04:06.400
of the available data
showed that,

00:04:06.400 --> 00:04:08.560
although outcomes were mixed,
on average,

00:04:08.560 --> 00:04:11.160
there were beneficial effects
for skin hydration,

00:04:11.160 --> 00:04:13.680
elasticity, and roughness.

00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:15.440
Despite some favorable results,

00:04:15.440 --> 00:04:17.240
much of the
dermatology community

00:04:17.240 --> 00:04:19.280
has remained skeptical,

00:04:19.280 --> 00:04:21.800
suggesting the collagen
supplement industry may just be

00:04:21.800 --> 00:04:25.800
flooding the zone with
poor quality, biased studies.

00:04:25.800 --> 00:04:27.240
The studies are often funded

00:04:27.240 --> 00:04:29.240
by collagen supplement
manufacturers,

00:04:29.240 --> 00:04:32.200
and the overall quality of
evidence is considered to be

00:04:32.200 --> 00:04:36.200
"limited, contradictory,"
"not particularly robust."

00:04:36.200 --> 00:04:38.240
A 2022 review entitled

00:04:38.240 --> 00:04:40.920
"Myths and media in oral
collagen supplementation

00:04:40.920 --> 00:04:42.440
for the skin, nails, and hair"

00:04:42.440 --> 00:04:45.920
in the Journal of Cosmetic
Dermatology concluded that

00:04:45.920 --> 00:04:47.320
"Dermatologists should be aware

00:04:47.320 --> 00:04:50.240
of the unsubstantiated
proclamations of collagen

00:04:50.240 --> 00:04:52.760
made by companies
[which] surpass any evidence

00:04:52.760 --> 00:04:54.640
currently supported
by the literature."

00:04:54.640 --> 00:04:56.400
And, given
the insufficient evidence,

00:04:56.400 --> 00:04:59.640
"collagen cannot be routinely
recommended at this time."

00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:03.040
Moreover, the evidence
is considered to be

00:05:03.040 --> 00:05:04.520
"particularly unconvincing"

00:05:04.520 --> 00:05:07.320
when compared to methods more
definitely shown to have

00:05:07.320 --> 00:05:09.480
a positive effect
on skin collagen,

00:05:09.480 --> 00:05:12.520
such as sunscreen use,
smoking cessation,

00:05:12.520 --> 00:05:14.960
and a healthy diet.

00:05:14.960 --> 00:05:15.720
In our next story,

00:05:15.720 --> 00:05:20.360
we see if collagen can beat out
placebo for knee osteoarthritis.

00:05:20.360 --> 00:05:22.240
Nearly a millennium ago,

00:05:22.240 --> 00:05:26.760
a medieval nun suggested eating
gelatin to reduce joint pain.

00:05:26.760 --> 00:05:29.680
Unfortunately, when it was put
to the test in a multicenter,

00:05:29.680 --> 00:05:31.720
randomized double-blind
placebo-controlled trial,

00:05:31.720 --> 00:05:33.880
it didn't work.

00:05:33.880 --> 00:05:37.000
A dozen years later, looking
at knee and hip osteoarthritis,

00:05:37.000 --> 00:05:38.880
there was still
no overall evidence

00:05:38.880 --> 00:05:42.040
for collagen supplements
working better than placebo.

00:05:42.040 --> 00:05:44.760
However, the latest compilation
of studies purported to show

00:05:44.760 --> 00:05:47.640
a benefit for improving
osteoarthritis symptoms.

00:05:47.640 --> 00:05:50.240
In one symptom index, it
appeared to help with stiffness,

00:05:50.240 --> 00:05:52.160
but not pain or disability.

00:05:52.160 --> 00:05:54.480
But, an overall
improvement in symptoms

00:05:54.480 --> 00:05:59.080
and a different index of pain
were statistically significant.

00:05:59.080 --> 00:06:01.760
If, however, you exclude
the smallest study,

00:06:01.760 --> 00:06:04.120
whose authors were employees
of a collagen company,

00:06:04.120 --> 00:06:07.080
all of the results
lost clinical importance,

00:06:07.080 --> 00:06:10.960
meaning the overall effects
were unnoticeably small.

00:06:10.960 --> 00:06:14.880
In fact, every single one was
funded by collagen companies,

00:06:14.880 --> 00:06:18.520
or directly run
by company employees.

00:06:18.520 --> 00:06:20.400
Another thing you'll
notice about these data

00:06:20.400 --> 00:06:23.320
is that with this one exception,

00:06:23.320 --> 00:06:26.880
the smaller the study,
the larger the effect.

00:06:26.880 --> 00:06:30.200
This pattern is a common sign
of publication bias,

00:06:30.200 --> 00:06:32.960
the tendency to do
a whole bunch of small trials,

00:06:32.960 --> 00:06:35.400
but publish only the ones
that work to your advantage

00:06:35.400 --> 00:06:37.960
and secretly shelve the rest.

00:06:37.960 --> 00:06:39.760
Coupled with all of
the conflicts of interest,

00:06:39.760 --> 00:06:40.760
concerns have been raised

00:06:40.760 --> 00:06:43.040
that additional small studies
may have been conducted

00:06:43.040 --> 00:06:45.640
but conveniently disappeared.

00:06:45.640 --> 00:06:48.320
And then, other fishy findings
are that smaller doses

00:06:48.320 --> 00:06:49.840
appeared to have larger effects,

00:06:49.840 --> 00:06:53.800
as did using it
for a shorter amount of time.

00:06:53.800 --> 00:06:56.240
Okay, but the last study
was published in 2016.

00:06:56.240 --> 00:06:58.440
What's happened since then?

00:06:58.440 --> 00:07:01.360
A comprehensive systematic
review published in 2022

00:07:01.360 --> 00:07:03.320
was surprised to note
that the last study done

00:07:03.320 --> 00:07:05.280
on collagen supplements
for rheumatoid arthritis

00:07:05.280 --> 00:07:07.240
was published
more than a decade ago,

00:07:07.240 --> 00:07:12.840
and the last osteoarthritis
study was still that 2016 one.

00:07:12.840 --> 00:07:13.960
Why?

00:07:13.960 --> 00:07:15.440
Well, one possible reason

00:07:15.440 --> 00:07:18.400
is the high incidence of
adverse side effects reported

00:07:18.400 --> 00:07:20.480
in those taking
collagen supplements,

00:07:20.480 --> 00:07:21.960
or another reason may be

00:07:21.960 --> 00:07:24.120
that they simply
don't work very well.

00:07:24.120 --> 00:07:27.520
I mean, if collagen company
executives truly believed

00:07:27.520 --> 00:07:29.280
they were selling
something that worked,

00:07:29.280 --> 00:07:31.120
why don't they do more studies?

00:07:31.120 --> 00:07:34.120
If they thought collagen had
a decent chance of working,

00:07:34.120 --> 00:07:36.480
they could make out
like bandits.

00:07:36.480 --> 00:07:38.880
The fact that they haven't
sponsored such a trial

00:07:38.880 --> 00:07:40.920
suggests they're
not so confident

00:07:40.920 --> 00:07:43.880
it would actually show benefit.

00:07:43.880 --> 00:07:47.640
One study has been published
since 2016, the biggest yet,

00:07:47.640 --> 00:07:49.920
with more than 150 people.

00:07:49.920 --> 00:07:51.400
Funded by a collagen company,

00:07:51.400 --> 00:07:54.160
researchers found
a significant drop in knee pain,

00:07:54.160 --> 00:07:56.480
and a significant
improvement in knee function

00:07:56.480 --> 00:07:59.240
among those randomized
to collagen supplements.

00:07:59.240 --> 00:08:01.720
But they also found a
significant drop in knee pain

00:08:01.720 --> 00:08:03.080
and improvement in knee function

00:08:03.080 --> 00:08:06.000
in those randomized
to the placebo,

00:08:06.000 --> 00:08:09.520
with no real difference
between them.

00:08:09.520 --> 00:08:11.960
So, the fact that
effectively a sugar pill

00:08:11.960 --> 00:08:14.240
worked as well as
the collagen supplements

00:08:14.240 --> 00:08:17.400
suggests that
collagen doesn't work.

00:08:17.400 --> 00:08:19.560
But that does raise
another point.

00:08:19.560 --> 00:08:21.440
If you look at what
was used for controls

00:08:21.440 --> 00:08:22.480
in the collagen studies,

00:08:22.480 --> 00:08:25.494
it was basically
just sugar or sawdust.

00:08:25.560 --> 00:08:28.520
So, even if collagen was found
to be helpful in some way,

00:08:28.520 --> 00:08:31.120
there is no proof
that collagen has any effects,

00:08:31.120 --> 00:08:33.600
beyond just being
a source of amino acids

00:08:33.600 --> 00:08:35.040
like any other protein.

00:08:35.040 --> 00:08:37.080
So, if any purported
collagen effect

00:08:37.080 --> 00:08:40.640
is just a matter of increased
amino acid availability,

00:08:40.640 --> 00:08:41.920
then why not just, like,

00:08:41.920 --> 00:08:45.080
have some extra hummus
or something?

00:08:45.080 --> 00:08:45.920
Finally today,

00:08:45.920 --> 00:08:51.720
we reveal which foods may be able to increase
collagen deposition and prevent wrinkles.

00:08:51.720 --> 00:08:54.560
As we age, the synthesis
of collagen decreases

00:08:54.560 --> 00:08:56.120
by about 1 percent a year,

00:08:56.120 --> 00:08:59.440
which may contribute
to the development of wrinkles.

00:09:00.080 --> 00:09:01.080
Though we don't have evidence

00:09:01.080 --> 00:09:05.240
that collagen is superior to any
other proteins for aging skin,

00:09:05.240 --> 00:09:08.480
those who do want to try it
are advised to contact

00:09:08.480 --> 00:09:12.120
the manufacturers
to clarify its source.

00:09:12.120 --> 00:09:15.320
Most collagen supplements don't
disclose this information

00:09:15.320 --> 00:09:16.760
and for good reason.

00:09:16.760 --> 00:09:19.600
Terrestrial sources
can include a witch's brew

00:09:19.600 --> 00:09:22.760
of kangaroo and rat tails,
duck feet, horse tendons,

00:09:22.760 --> 00:09:25.240
alligator bones,
and frog skin.

00:09:25.240 --> 00:09:28.480
Aquatic sources are mainly
from fish skins, bones, heads,

00:09:28.480 --> 00:09:30.760
scales, fins, and entrails.

00:09:30.760 --> 00:09:33.360
Recommended questions
for manufacturers include

00:09:33.360 --> 00:09:35.280
"What measures were used
to protect against

00:09:35.280 --> 00:09:37.960
contamination or adulteration?

00:09:37.960 --> 00:09:42.000
If sourced from fish,
were low-mercury fish used?

00:09:42.000 --> 00:09:43.640
If sourced from cows,

00:09:43.640 --> 00:09:45.400
what steps were taken
to ensure that

00:09:45.400 --> 00:09:48.000
no brain or nervous system
matter was included,

00:09:48.000 --> 00:09:50.560
in order to prevent
prion disease?"

00:09:50.560 --> 00:09:51.640
In the United States,

00:09:51.640 --> 00:09:54.800
collagen is exempt
from FDA prohibitions

00:09:54.800 --> 00:09:57.760
against using risky tissues
like brains--

00:09:57.760 --> 00:10:00.320
prohibitions in place
to protect consumers

00:10:00.320 --> 00:10:03.280
against bovine spongiform
encephalopathy,

00:10:03.280 --> 00:10:05.760
also known as mad cow disease.

00:10:05.760 --> 00:10:08.360
So, for food safety,
religious, ethical,

00:10:08.360 --> 00:10:10.240
or allergy-related reasons,

00:10:10.240 --> 00:10:13.800
there have been calls
for non-animal alternatives.

00:10:13.800 --> 00:10:15.680
For example, 2 to 4 percent
of the population

00:10:15.680 --> 00:10:18.520
is allergic to bovine collagen.

00:10:18.520 --> 00:10:20.560
To solve the mad cow conundrum,

00:10:20.560 --> 00:10:22.560
there have been calls
to genetically engineer

00:10:22.560 --> 00:10:24.000
cattle without prions

00:10:24.000 --> 00:10:27.600
to "offer a safe source
of collagen-based materials."

00:10:27.600 --> 00:10:30.560
But why not just get
plants to make it?

00:10:30.560 --> 00:10:34.080
A technique has been perfected
to produce collagen from plants,

00:10:34.080 --> 00:10:37.520
but it has not yet reached
commercial viability.

00:10:37.520 --> 00:10:40.460
It's hard to beat
the cost of feet.

00:10:40.460 --> 00:10:44.400
But what about all of the vegan
collagen products on the market?

00:10:44.400 --> 00:10:47.240
If you look closely,
you'll see the small print:

00:10:47.240 --> 00:10:51.120
vegan collagen builder
or vegan collagen booster.

00:10:51.120 --> 00:10:52.840
It's not actually collagen,

00:10:52.840 --> 00:10:55.480
since collagen
is made by animals,

00:10:55.480 --> 00:10:57.920
but you're an animal,
you make collagen.

00:10:57.920 --> 00:10:58.760
So they're suggesting

00:10:58.760 --> 00:11:01.360
their supplements
can boost production,

00:11:01.360 --> 00:11:02.280
but since it appears

00:11:02.280 --> 00:11:04.320
none of them have actually
been put to the test,

00:11:04.320 --> 00:11:06.400
I assume it's all
a load of bunk.

00:11:06.400 --> 00:11:08.000
But there are some
foods and nutrients

00:11:08.000 --> 00:11:10.640
that have been investigated.

00:11:10.640 --> 00:11:14.960
Researchers burned the butts
of 20 women with a UV lamp

00:11:14.960 --> 00:11:16.440
before and after half of them

00:11:16.440 --> 00:11:19.040
ate three tablespoons
of tomato paste each day

00:11:19.040 --> 00:11:20.440
for three months.

00:11:20.440 --> 00:11:23.720
And there was a significant
reduction in MMP-1 levels

00:11:23.720 --> 00:11:26.440
in the derrieres of those
who had been randomized

00:11:26.440 --> 00:11:28.040
to the tomatoes.

00:11:28.040 --> 00:11:30.480
(That's a major
collagen-eating enzyme

00:11:30.480 --> 00:11:33.760
that plays a role in skin aging
caused by the sun.)

00:11:33.760 --> 00:11:36.800
Butt biopsies also show
that the amount of beta-carotene

00:11:36.800 --> 00:11:38.480
found in about
one and a third cup

00:11:38.480 --> 00:11:40.800
of sweet potato
can boost collagen production

00:11:40.800 --> 00:11:44.320
as much as four-fold
within three months.

00:11:44.320 --> 00:11:46.160
To test the effects of greens,

00:11:46.160 --> 00:11:48.040
Korean researchers
randomized older women

00:11:48.040 --> 00:11:50.280
to the amount of chlorophyll
found in a few tablespoons

00:11:50.280 --> 00:11:51.960
of cooked spinach a day.

00:11:51.960 --> 00:11:53.960
After three months,
skin biopsies showed

00:11:53.960 --> 00:11:56.720
a significant increase
in collagen production,

00:11:56.720 --> 00:11:59.280
accompanied by an increase
in skin elasticity

00:11:59.280 --> 00:12:02.960
and a decrease
in facial wrinkles.

00:12:02.960 --> 00:12:05.600
Unfortunately, this was just
a before-and-after comparison

00:12:05.600 --> 00:12:08.600
to baseline study
with no control group,

00:12:08.600 --> 00:12:09.400
but there have been

00:12:09.400 --> 00:12:11.400
placebo-controlled
randomized trials,

00:12:11.400 --> 00:12:14.200
and of kale no less!

00:12:14.200 --> 00:12:17.280
Compared to placebo, there was
a significant improvement

00:12:17.280 --> 00:12:19.720
in a noninvasive measure
of skin collagen status

00:12:19.720 --> 00:12:21.600
after 10 months
of being randomized

00:12:21.600 --> 00:12:24.000
to an extract of curly kale.

00:12:24.000 --> 00:12:26.480
The researchers concluded
that "a healthy lifestyle

00:12:26.480 --> 00:12:28.400
including a diet
rich in carotenoids

00:12:28.400 --> 00:12:29.960
is the best prevention strategy

00:12:29.960 --> 00:12:32.880
against premature skin aging."

00:12:32.880 --> 00:12:35.080
That led to dermatology
journal commentaries

00:12:35.080 --> 00:12:37.520
with titles like "Eat plenty
of green leafy vegetables

00:12:37.520 --> 00:12:41.240
for protection from
the pro-aging rays of the sun,"

00:12:41.240 --> 00:12:43.880
but might it actually be
more than that?

00:12:43.880 --> 00:12:47.120
Sulforaphane, the cruciferous
compound in kale and broccoli,

00:12:47.120 --> 00:12:49.160
may be able to mobilize
cellular defenses

00:12:49.160 --> 00:12:52.360
that protect skin
against damage by UV radiation,

00:12:52.360 --> 00:12:54.520
as well as mitigate
the premature skin aging

00:12:54.520 --> 00:12:57.440
induced by air pollution
particulate matter,

00:12:57.440 --> 00:13:00.760
though this was on skin cells
in a Petri dish.

00:13:00.760 --> 00:13:03.920
Soybean compounds can increase
collagen deposition

00:13:03.920 --> 00:13:08.800
and prevent wrinkles in mice,
but what about in people?

00:13:08.800 --> 00:13:10.240
Given improvements
of skin aging

00:13:10.240 --> 00:13:12.600
seen with menopausal
hormone therapy

00:13:12.600 --> 00:13:14.200
but concerned
about the side effects,

00:13:14.200 --> 00:13:17.360
researchers in Brazil
tried giving phytohormones,

00:13:17.360 --> 00:13:20.560
plant hormones in the form
of soy phytoestrogens.

00:13:20.560 --> 00:13:22.120
Twenty-nine
postmenopausal women

00:13:22.120 --> 00:13:24.560
were given the amount
in four servings a day

00:13:24.560 --> 00:13:27.000
of soy foods for six months.

00:13:27.000 --> 00:13:28.840
Unfortunately,
there was no control group,

00:13:28.840 --> 00:13:31.200
but they did take skin biopsies
before and after

00:13:31.200 --> 00:13:34.360
and found a significant
improvement in skin thickness,

00:13:34.360 --> 00:13:35.960
along with increased
collagen content

00:13:35.960 --> 00:13:37.440
and elastic fibers.

00:13:37.440 --> 00:13:39.880
The biopsies
of 25 out of 29 women

00:13:39.880 --> 00:13:42.280
showed an increase in collagen.

00:13:42.280 --> 00:13:44.960
You could also throw in
some vitamin C-rich foods,

00:13:44.960 --> 00:13:47.560
as vitamin C appears
to stimulate collagen production

00:13:47.560 --> 00:13:49.440
from human skin cells.

00:13:49.440 --> 00:13:52.160
Defective wound healing
has long been known

00:13:52.160 --> 00:13:54.360
as a major manifestation
of scurvy,

00:13:54.360 --> 00:13:56.480
the vitamin C-deficiency
disease.

00:13:56.480 --> 00:13:59.680
In fact, a remarkable series
of grisly experiments

00:13:59.680 --> 00:14:02.120
performed on British
conscientious objectors

00:14:02.120 --> 00:14:03.480
during World War II

00:14:03.480 --> 00:14:06.120
involved being subjected
to cuts and stab wounds

00:14:06.120 --> 00:14:08.080
after being randomized
to different levels

00:14:08.080 --> 00:14:09.480
of vitamin C.

00:14:09.480 --> 00:14:10.400
The researchers found

00:14:10.400 --> 00:14:12.160
that the average
daily vitamin C intake

00:14:12.160 --> 00:14:16.280
to prevent weak scar formation
was about 95 milligrams,

00:14:16.280 --> 00:14:19.320
which is actually higher than
the current recommendations

00:14:19.320 --> 00:14:23.080
but nothing you can't get
from half a bell pepper.

00:14:23.080 --> 00:14:25.600
Vitamin B12 also appears
to be necessary

00:14:25.600 --> 00:14:27.840
for maximum collagen synthesis,

00:14:27.840 --> 00:14:30.120
which may explain why
collagen synthesis rates

00:14:30.120 --> 00:14:33.360
appear to be about 10 percent
lower in vegetarians,

00:14:33.360 --> 00:14:35.200
because the prevalence
of B12 deficiency

00:14:35.200 --> 00:14:39.320
among those eating
plant-based diets is so high.

00:14:39.320 --> 00:14:41.600
That's one of many reasons
why it's critically important

00:14:41.600 --> 00:14:44.320
that everyone consuming
a plant-based diet

00:14:44.320 --> 00:14:49.114
ensures a regular, reliable
source of vitamin B12.

